  "attributionNote": "The short treatise was transmitted as a chapter of the Book of Rites and was taken up as a separate classic by the Cheng brothers and Zhu Xi, who attributed its text to Confucius and its commentary to his disciple Zengzi. Its composition is usually dated to the Warring States period.",
  "description": "The Great Learning is the shortest of the Four Books and the one that sets out a programme: to illustrate illustrious virtue, to renovate the people, and to rest in the highest excellence. Its eight steps run from the investigation of things through sincerity and the cultivation of the person to the regulation of the family, the ordering of the state and peace under heaven, and Legge prints the classic with the commentary that accompanies each part.",
  "historicalContext": "The text circulated inside the Book of Rites until Zhu Xi printed it with the Analects, the Doctrine of the Mean and Mencius as the Four Books, after which it was studied by every candidate for the examinations. Legge translated it for the first volume of The Chinese Classics (1893) with the Chinese text and his own commentary beside it.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete in Legge's translation, streamed from the Internet Sacred Text Archive at read time and read as one printed page of the volume, with Legge's paragraphs indexed. The page's Chinese text and the translator's notes belong to the volume's apparatus and are not part of the work.",
